Q: How do I correctly render glyphs in dataTables PDF export? A: <p>Here are some partial answers:</p>
<hr />
<p><strong><em>1) How do I find an appropriate font to correctly render the two unicode characters that I need?</em></strong></p>
<p>All of the &quot;free commercial use&quot; downloadable fonts I found in a quick search did not include the <code>★</code> and <code>☆</code> symbols.</p>
<p>That includes the font it looks like you are trying to use in your question, as well. Even though it is &quot;free&quot; and &quot;arial&quot;, it still has a limited set of Unicode characters, symbols and glyphs. The Unicode <a href="https://www.fileformat.info/info/unicode/block/miscellaneous_symbols/images.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Miscellaneous Symbols</a> block is not included.</p>
<p>You can use this <a href="https://befonts.com/arial-font.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">free for personal use</a> font - but that is obviously not suitable for commercial use.</p>
<p>It does include the stars, though:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/WViD1HwX.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/WViD1HwX.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<hr />
<p><strong><em>2) How do I properly incorporate it so that I can resolve my js error and successfully export the correctly rendered PDF?</em></strong></p>
<p>The JavaScript code in your question does not handle the downloaded binary font file data correctly, resulting in an invalid base-64 string.</p>
<p>jQuery does not have a straightforward built-in way to handle binary files, although you can search Stack Overflow for some approaches.</p>
<p>In this case, I find it simpler to use pure JavaScript, not jQuery's <code>ajax</code>.</p>
<p>Specifically, the following uses <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileReader/readAsDataURL" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>FleReader.readAsDataUrl</code></a> because when using this approach:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>the <code>result</code> attribute contains... the file's data as a base64 encoded string.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is what we want!</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var req = new XMLHttpRequest();
req.open(&quot;GET&quot;, fontUrl, true);
req.responseType = &quot;blob&quot;;
req.onload = function (event) {
  const blob = req.response;

  var reader = new FileReader();
  reader.readAsDataURL(blob); 
  reader.onloadend = function() {
    const base64data = reader.result.replace(/^data:.+;base64,/, '');            
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The above code is based on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18650168/convert-blob-to-base64/18650249#18650249">this answer</a> to a similar question.</p>
<hr />
<p>However, this still does not solve the PDF font problem because (as noted above) the specific font you appear to be using does not contain the glyphs you need.</p>
<p>But despite that, here is the full DataTables code you can copy and run for yourself.</p>
<p>(This is using the latest version of DataTables (2.0.0), with the newly introduced <a href="https://datatables.net/reference/option/layout" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>layout</code></a> option. You may be using an older version, without that option - but that does not matter for the <em>specific</em> base 64 problem you are facing. You can use the old DataTables <code>dom</code> option, if you prefer.)</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Demo&lt;/title&gt;

  &lt;link href=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/2.0.5/css/dataTables.dataTables.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;&gt;
  &lt;link href=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/css/buttons.dataTables.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;&gt;

  &lt;script src=&quot;https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.0.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdfmake/0.2.7/pdfmake.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdfmake/0.2.7/vfs_fonts.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/2.0.5/js/dataTables.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/js/dataTables.buttons.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/js/buttons.html5.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; type=&quot;text/css&quot; href=&quot;https://datatables.net/media/css/site-examples.css&quot;&gt;  

&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;

&lt;div style=&quot;margin: 20px;&quot;&gt;

    &lt;table id=&quot;example&quot; class=&quot;display dataTable cell-border&quot; style=&quot;width:100%&quot;&gt;
        &lt;thead&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Position&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Office in Country&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Age&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Start date&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Salary&lt;/th&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/thead&gt;
        &lt;tbody&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Tiger Nixon&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;System Architect&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Edinburgh&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;61 ☆&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;2011/04/25&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;$320,800&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Garrett Winters&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Accountant&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Tokyo&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;63 ★&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;2011/07/25&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;$170,750&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Ashton Cox&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Junior &quot;Technical&quot; Author&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;San Francisco&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;66&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;2009/01/12&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;$86,000&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script&gt;

$(document).ready(function() {

  $('#example').DataTable({
    layout: {
      topStart: {
        buttons: [{
          extend: 'pdfHtml5',
          customize: function ( doc ) {
            processDoc( doc );
          }
        }]
      }
    }
  });

} );

  function processDoc( doc ) {

    const fontUrl = 'https://static.miraheze.org/argobdpwiki/5/5d/FreeSans-LrmZ.ttf';

    var req = new XMLHttpRequest();
    req.open(&quot;GET&quot;, fontUrl, true);
    req.responseType = &quot;blob&quot;;
    req.onload = function (event) {
      const blob = req.response;

      var reader = new FileReader();
      reader.readAsDataURL(blob); 
      reader.onloadend = function() {
        const base64data = reader.result.replace(/^data:.+;base64,/, '');            
        pdfMake.vfs[&quot;FreeSans.ttf&quot;] = base64data;
        pdfMake.fonts = {
          Roboto: {
            normal: 'Roboto-Regular.ttf',
            bold: 'Roboto-Medium.ttf',
            italics: 'Roboto-Italic.ttf',
            bolditalics: 'Roboto-MediumItalic.ttf'
          },
          FreeSans: {
            normal: 'FreeSans.ttf',
            bold: 'FreeSans.ttf',
            italics: 'FreeSans.ttf',
            bolditalics: 'FreeSans.ttf'
          }
        };
        // modify the PDF to use a different default font:
        doc.defaultStyle.font = &quot;FreeSans&quot;;
      }
    };

    req.send();

  }

&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You can see the URL I am using in the above code:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const fontUrl = 'https://static.miraheze.org/argobdpwiki/5/5d/FreeSans-LrmZ.ttf';
</code></pre>
<p>If you find a font file with the glyphs you need and with a suitable license, you can change my demo URL to the one you want to use instead.</p>
<p>(You can obviously host the relevant font file yourself - it doesn't have to be an external URL, like my example. It can be a URL specific to your application.)</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Follow-Up Notes</strong></p>
<p>Based on the comment, I took a look at that font, and I think there may be some issues with it - and with my code, also... so my apologies for that.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://befonts.com/arial-font.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">font</a> lets you scroll through a list of all the symbols it contains (the &quot;view all glyphs&quot; buttons) - and I do not see the stars in that list.</p>
<p>So, I generated a base-64 string for the main font file (<code>arial.ttf</code>). Then I created a simplified version of the DataTables code, where I do not use the provided <code>vfs_fonts.js</code> file. Instead I create the objects provided by that file in the code directly, and I use the hard-coded base-64 string - just to make sure we really are loading and using that font for the PDF:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Export to PDF&lt;/title&gt;

  &lt;link href=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/2.0.5/css/dataTables.dataTables.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;&gt;
  &lt;link href=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/css/buttons.dataTables.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;&gt;

  &lt;script src=&quot;https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.0.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdfmake/0.2.7/pdfmake.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdfmake/0.2.7/vfs_fonts.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/2.0.5/js/dataTables.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/js/dataTables.buttons.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.datatables.net/buttons/3.0.2/js/buttons.html5.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; type=&quot;text/css&quot; href=&quot;https://datatables.net/media/css/site-examples.css&quot;&gt;

  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Material+Symbols+Outlined:opsz,wght,FILL,GRAD@20..48,100..700,0..1,-50..200&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Material+Symbols+Outlined:opsz,wght,FILL,GRAD@24,400,0,0&quot; /&gt;  


&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;

&lt;div style=&quot;margin: 20px;&quot;&gt;

&lt;table id=&quot;example&quot; class=&quot;display nowrap dataTable cell-border&quot; style=&quot;width:100%&quot;&gt;
        &lt;thead&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Data&lt;/th&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/thead&gt;
        &lt;tbody&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;ABC ʓ ★ ☆ ☻ ☼ ֍ ػ ۞ ‰&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;

  this.pdfMake = this.pdfMake || {}; 
  this.pdfMake.vfs = {
    &quot;arial.ttf&quot;: &quot;AAEAA...hZQA=&quot; // very long string here!
  };

  $(document).ready(function() {
    $('#example').DataTable({
    layout: {
      topStart: {
        buttons: [{
          extend: 'pdfHtml5',
          customize: function ( doc ) {
            processDoc( doc );
          }
        }]
      }
    }
  });
  });

  function processDoc(doc) {

    pdfMake.fonts = {
      arial: {
        normal: 'arial.ttf',
        bold: 'arial.ttf',
        italics: 'arial.ttf',
        bolditalics: 'arial.ttf'
      }
    };
    // modify the PDF to use a different default font:
    doc.defaultStyle.font = &quot;arial&quot;;
    var i = 1;
  }

&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In the DataTable, I see all the symbols in my test data:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/i2Fiacj8.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/i2Fiacj8.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>But in the PDF, I see everything except the two symbols you want to see:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/M6DunuXp.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/M6DunuXp.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>So, unfortunately, it really does look as if the stars are not part of that font set, after all - even though they were shown in the preview.</p>
<p>The PDF is definitely using the font, as shown when using Acrobat and looking at the fonts used:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/mLrR9RmD.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/mLrR9RmD.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>I don't know how to investigate this further, but maybe these notes can help you to ask a more targeted question - or at least improve the JavaScript, if you don't want to pre-generate (and hard-code) the base-64 string, using my simplified approach.</p>

Q: Scroll the page on drag with jQuery A: <p>I updated Rory's jQuery plugin with the following improvements:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>allow chaining of jQuery functions</p>
</li>
<li><p>default cursor value set only at init</p>
</li>
<li><p>alow individual elements to be scrolled not only the entire document</p>
</li>
<li><p>set cursor values for given element and its direct children</p>
</li>
<li><p>saves the original cursor value in data store</p>
</li>
<li><p>restore the orginal cursor value from data when drag is finished</p>
</li>
<li><p>prevent other mouse actions (on children) to be fired during drag</p>
</li>
<li><p>restore other mouse actions (on children) to be fired after drag is finished</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>// the semi-colon before function invocation is a safety net against concatenated
// scripts and/or other plugins which may not be closed properly.
;(function ( $, window, document, undefined ) {

    // undefined is used here as the undefined global variable in ECMAScript 3 is
    // mutable (ie. it can be changed by someone else). undefined isn't really being
    // passed in so we can ensure the value of it is truly undefined. In ES5, undefined
    // can no longer be modified.

    // window is passed through as local variable rather than global
    // as this (slightly) quickens the resolution process and can be more efficiently
    // minified (especially when both are regularly referenced in your plugin).

    $.fn.dragScroll = function(options) {
        var settings = $.extend({
            scrollVertical: true,
            scrollHorizontal: true,
            cursor: null
        }, options);

        var clicked = false, clickY, clickX;

        // cursor value is null
        if (!settings.cursor) {
            // move - vertical and horizontal
            if (settings.scrollVertical &amp;&amp; settings.scrollHorizontal) settings.cursor = 'move';
            // row-resize - vertical
            else if (settings.scrollVertical) settings.cursor = 'row-resize';
            // col-resize - horizontal
            else if (settings.scrollHorizontal) settings.cursor = 'col-resize';
        };

        // set or reset cursor value
        var setCursor = function(elArray, e, cursor=null) {
            // in reverse order, parent last (due to css precendense)
            $(elArray.get().reverse()).each(function(i, el) {
                // jQuery el object
                var $el = $(el);
                if (cursor) {
                    // xy coordinates are different (mousemove)
                    if ((e.pageX != clickX) || (e.pageY != clickY)) {
                        // set new value
                        $el.css('cursor', cursor);
                    // xy are the same (mousedown)
                    } else {
                        // get old style value
                        var oldCursor = $el.css('cursor');
                        // store old value in data
                        $el.data('oldCursor', oldCursor);
                    };
                // value is null
                } else {
                    // get old value from data
                    var oldCursor = $el.data('oldCursor');
                    // $el.removeData('oldCursor');
                    // cursor is auto or undefined, remove style declaration
                    if (oldCursor == 'auto' || !oldCursor) $el.css('cursor', '');
                    // set old style value
                    else $el.css('cursor', oldCursor);
                };
            });
        };

        // scroll event to position
        var updateScrollPos = function(e, el) {
            // if scrollVertical enabled, scroll to (click Y - event Y) coordinates
            settings.scrollVertical &amp;&amp; el.scrollTop(el.scrollTop() + (clickY - e.pageY));
            // if scrollHorizontal enabled, scroll to (click X - event X) coordinates
            settings.scrollHorizontal &amp;&amp; el.scrollLeft(el.scrollLeft() + (clickX - e.pageX));
        };

        // return jquery to continue object chain
        return this.each(function (i, el) {
            // jQuery el object
            var $this = $(el);
            // jQuery children object of el
            // if element is just created, there are no children
            var $children = $this.children();

            // only on given element
            $this.on({
                // during drag action
                'mousemove': function(e) {
                    // if click flag is set
                    if (clicked) {
                        // scroll event to position
                        updateScrollPos(e, $this);
                        // prevent other (default) pointer events
                        $children.css('pointer-events', 'none');
                    // set cursor to element and its children
                    setCursor($this.add($children), e, settings.cursor);
                    };

                // start drag action
                }, 'mousedown': function(e) {
                    // set click flag
                    clicked = true;
                    // store X and Y click coordinates
                    clickY = e.pageY; clickX = e.pageX;
                    // jQuery children object of el
                    // update the children aray just before the drag
                    $children = $this.children();
                    // save cursor to element and its children data
                    setCursor($this.add($children), e, settings.cursor);
                    // prevent dragging
                    e.preventDefault();
                },
            });

            // anywhere in the document
            $(document).on({
                // finish drag action
                'mouseup': function(e) {
                    // set click flag
                    clicked = false;
                    // clear cursor to element and its children
                    setCursor($this.add($children), e, null);
                    // allow other (default) pointer events
                    $children.css('pointer-events', '');
                    // $children.css('pointer-events', 'all');
                },
            });
        });
    };
}(jQuery, window, document));
</code></pre>

Q: Fastest way to gather bits (similar to std::copy_if) A: <p>Brian Kernighan's way:</p>
<pre><code>size_t gather_bits(size_t source, size_t mask) {
    size_t result = 0;
    size_t bitMask = 1;
    size_t nextMask;
    while (mask) {
        nextMask = mask &amp; mask - 1;
        if (source &amp; (mask ^ nextMask)) result |= bitMask;
        bitMask &lt;&lt;= 1;
        mask = nextMask;
    }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>At each iteration, the lowest set bit of <code>mask</code> mask is reset. The new value is written to <code>nextMask</code>. The <code>mask ^ nextMask</code> operation allows us to determine the bit that has just been reset. The <code>bitMask</code> variable contains the current bit to pack into <code>result</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p>Without <code>if</code> statement (branchless):</p>
<pre><code>size_t gather_bits(size_t source, size_t mask) {
    size_t result = 0;
    size_t bitMask = 1;
    size_t nextMask, cond;
    while (mask) {
        nextMask = mask &amp; mask - 1;
        cond = source &amp; (mask ^ nextMask);
        result |= bitMask &amp; -(cond != 0);
        bitMask &lt;&lt;= 1
        mask = nextMask;
    }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>-(cond != 0)</code> expression returns the value where all the bits are set if <code>cond</code> contains at least one set bit. With it, we can reset <code>bitMask &amp; -(cond != 0)</code> mask when no bit setting is required. Which saves us from branching.</p>
